The material was received as a casted block from OCAS, Belgium, cut into smaller pieces of 60 × 80 × 120 mm, and rolled down to the thickness of 12 mm to enable the preparation of two kinds of cylindrical specimens, Ø6x36 mm for the evaluation of M S  temperature and Ø6x9 mm specimens for the physical simulation of bainite transformation behaviour above and below the experimentally determined M S  temperature. Both the evaluation of M S  temperature and the bainite formation experiments were conducted in the Gleeble simulator. For determining the M S  temperature, a series of quenching experiments were conducted with cooling rates in the range of 5–50 ° Cs −1 . After quenching, the specimens were immediately reheated to 900 °C to estimate the martensite fraction through linear analysis using lever rule. Physical simulation experiments were divided into three different processing routes, one (1) with, another (2) without low-temperature ausforming treatment and a third (3) with ausforming without bainitizing at isothermal holding temperature (see  Table 2 
                      ). Three of the five isothermal holding temperatures (235, 255, and 275 °C) comprising different fractions of primary martensite were selected below M S  temperature (279 °C) for bainitizing treatment and for comparison, two temperatures (300 and 325 °C) were selected above M S  temperature as illustrated in  Fig. 1 
                       and in  Table 2 . The samples were reheated at 10 °Cs −1  up to 1100 °C, held for 2 min and subsequently cooled at a cooling rate of 30 °C/s to the isothermal holding temperature (T h ), followed by holding for 60 min. The samples were finally cooled at 30 °C/s down to RT. The ausformed samples underwent a similar thermal cycle, except that prior to isothermal holding, a single-hit uniaxial compression was applied to reach a true strain of 0.5 at a strain rate of 0.5 s −1  at temperature T def + h  (see  Table 2 ). Dilatation was recorded along the entire cycle. For clarifying the plausible phase transformation characteristics, the relative change in diameter was plotted as a function of temperature and holding time.

Area fractions of martensitic laths and bainite plates in ND and AF samples as a function of corresponding misorientation angles they were bounded with were extracted from EBSD data and are presented in  Fig. 15 
                            a and b, respectively. Most of the boundaries between RA and BF or RA and M appeared to have misorientation in the range of 41–47° (see  Fig. 16 
                            ), the average being approximately 45°, which is close to the Nishiyama-Wassermann (NW) relationship of 45.99° and is in a good agreement with the findings reported in the literature [ 11 , 60 ]. Generally, a high fraction of boundaries with 45° misorientation correlated well with the high f RA , for instance in the case of ND-325 (17%) and AF-325 (23%) specimens. In ND samples, the fraction of 41–47° boundaries was in line with the corresponding f RA  at 235, 255, 275, and 325 °C (10, 15, 17 and 17%), whereas ND-300 with relatively low f RA  (12%), but a high fraction of 41–47° boundaries did not follow the trend. Besides, the fractions of 41–47° misorientation boundaries in ND-255, ND-275, ND-300 did not remarkably vary from those of AF-255, AF-275, and AF-300 specimens despite the remarkable difference in the f RA  of these samples. At the same time, the deformation significantly reduced the fractions of 41–47° misorientation boundaries in samples isothermally held at 235 and 325 °C, well below the levels observed in corresponding ND samples, while causing an opposite influence on f RA  at the two temperatures. However, it should be emphasized that EBSD may not be able to index all the fine retained austenite (<80 nm) due to the resolution limit of the device. Thus, the actual fraction of RA-BF or RA-M boundaries may be somewhat higher than shown here, especially at low transformation temperatures, as the average size of phase constituents decreased with decreasing transformation temperature. Nevertheless, the effect of deformation on the misorientation distribution in ferritic phase constituents indicated that the low-temperature deformation promoted the fraction of low angle boundaries and suppressed high angle boundaries, especially 7.5°.
